2026-09-28 — K02j: direct-null comparison selection and MIR null transport.

The hot-path assembly audit found pointer-null tests materializing a separate
zero register. The shape RED now selects the existing 64-bit CMP-immediate
only for EQ/NE with a directly defined ptr.null, on either side. Other pointer
facts, casts, exposed addresses and nonlocal data are not reclassified. The
other operand retains its pointer identity in the interpreter. Existing
branch fusion, guards, operand evaluation and dead-definition handling remain.

The C reduction also exposed a correctness failure BEFORE the selection fix:
C-HIR/EvoIR return 0, but MIR rejects a typed null-pointer store with E3013
invalid-pointer-representation. ptr.null already lowers to MovImm(0), while
the old typed store accepts only tagged pointer variants. Atomic stores and
pointer exchange/compare-exchange have the same operand-conversion gap.
One shared conversion now recognizes that null representation in all three
paths. It does not expose an object/function, admit nonzero raw provenance,
or turn an ordinary integer-zero store into a pointer fragment. Existing
nonzero raw-MIR errors and atomic/volatile events remain checked independently.
This interpreter correction is not a new atomic width/order/source contract.

Ten focused MIR/CLI tests PASS. Coverage includes 20 null/object/exposed/
function comparison shapes, both EQ/NE operand orders, explicit expected
values and EvoIR/MIR agreement; branch flags, cast controls, text round-trip,
allocation verification and 40 Linux/Darwin assembler combinations. Ordinary
null transport covers both pointer types with/without volatile (4 cases).
Atomic store/exchange/CAS cover both types with/without volatile (12 cases),
including exact world/event equality. Six ordinary-store and six atomic
negative controls preserve nonzero/raw-byte rejection. The permanent C test
passes O0/O2 C-HIR, EvoIR and MIR, including once-only operand side effects.

An initial combined assembler fixture accidentally reused allocation origin 0
across functions and was correctly rejected. Distinct origins repair the test;
targeted.log and verify.json retain that failed attempt. The first ordinary-null
gate precedes the discovered atomic follow-up and is not the final gate.

Final pinned Rust/Cargo 1.97.1 affected five-crate gate:
  1093 PASS, 0 FAIL, 4 historical ignored; Clippy -D warnings and fmt PASS.
  tools/gate-changed.sh 49adf32 --offline -j 2
All source changes were fixed for that final gate and release build.

Fourteen sealed ARM64 link/runs PASS at O0/O2: EvoCC/GCC14/Clang19 whole
programs, and both caller/callee directions for EvoCC versus each reference.
Reference sources use -Wall -Wextra -Werror. The native C fixture includes
ordinary null arrays, function pointers, atomic null store/load and legacy
pointer CAS/exchange. These are single-thread semantic checks, not a new
concurrency-memory-model proof. Container network is disabled and the image
digest is verified before and after execution.

